  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Fog likely tonight, especially Downeast. Fog \ncould be dense enough to impact travel especially near the \ncoast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe marine layer this morning is expected to burn off by late\nmorning. However, some fog could move onshore along the coast\nduring the day today with the SSE winds. Although temps will be\ncooler today, the southerly flow will continue to bring moisture\ninto the region. In addition, the next system will push more\nmoisture into the area ahead of the rain. These parameters will\nprep the area for fog tonight as the winds calm and the temps\ncool. Greater low level moisture will allow for the marine layer\nto move into the coast again. Unsure how far north the marine\nlayer will reach, but the coast will see reduced visibility.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain Thursday into Thursday night, with higher\ntotals Downeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe closed low pressure system is expected to slow over New\nEngland before tracking NE across the Gulf of Maine. The\nensembles and deterministic models show a better agreement with\nthe higher rain amounts staying in the south while the north\ncould see very little rain in the form of isolated showers.\nRainfall amounts seem to have decreased further, however, due to\nthe possible showery nature to the rain, still uncertain to the\namounts. There is some potential for a thunderstorm Thursday\nafternoon, but not anticipating severe conditions.",
